 # Lightwork Helm chart
 
-Production-oriented chart for the Lightwork dashboard (web UI + API + webhooks).
+Helm chart for the Lightwork dashboard (web UI + API + webhooks). The default
+values support local/evaluation installs; the separate production overlay
+enforces the release invariants described below.
+
+## Production install (fail closed)
+
+Pre-provision a Kubernetes Secret through your secret manager or
+ExternalSecrets controller. It must contain the deployment's database,
+identity, provider, encryption, audit-signing, and dedicated metrics credentials
+as applicable. Production requires a random `MAVERICK_METRICS_TOKEN` of at
+least 32 bytes; do not reuse a dashboard/operator or human SSO credential.
+At minimum the mounted Secret needs `MAVERICK_PG_DSN` (verified TLS),
+`MAVERICK_KMS_KEY_ID`, `VAULT_TOKEN`, the selected identity secrets,
+`MAVERICK_AUDIT_SIGNING_KEY_WRAPPED`, and
+`MAVERICK_METRICS_TOKEN`. For the report-only production-onboarding evaluator,
+the same allowlisted Secret must contain
+`MAVERICK_PRODUCTION_ONBOARDING_OBSERVATIONS`: one signed strict-JSON
+observations document prepared by the independently trusted evidence authority.
+The first model profile also requires
+`VLLM_API_KEY`, even when the self-hosted gateway accepts a deployment-scoped
+sentinel rather than a vendor credential. Kubernetes exposes Secret volumes through mutable
+symlinks, which the strict runtime custody reader refuses. A non-root init
+container therefore snapshots the read-only projection into a memory-backed
+volume as owner-only mode-0400 regular files. Only that immutable snapshot is
+mounted at `/run/secrets/lightwork`; the application container never mounts
+the projection or bulk-copies the Secret into its environment. After any
+mounted-secret rotation, roll the pod to validate and activate one new snapshot.
+Production also requires a public `secret.rotationGeneration`; change it on
+every Secret generation so an in-place Secret update changes the pod template
+and cannot leave the staged snapshot stale. It is an opaque sequence, not a
+credential, resource version, or secret digest.
+
+Production also requires a **separate** pre-provisioned migration Secret with
+only `MAVERICK_PG_MIGRATION_DSN` and
+`MAVERICK_MIGRATION_AUTHORIZATION`. The serving Deployment never mounts it.
+Follow
+[`production-migration-safety.md`](../../docs/enterprise/production-migration-safety.md),
+compute SHA-256 over the authorization file's exact bytes, and keep all writers
+drained through the hook and runtime verification.
+
+Before rendering, obtain the exact 40-hex source revision and SHA-256 digests
+of the checked-out tag's `release/production-onboarding-contract.json` and
+`release/release-state.json` from the signed release packet. Select a stable
+deployment URN and independently custodied Ed25519 authority key ID/public key.
+These are operator trust pins, not values copied from the observations. The
+operator must also select exactly one `identity.mode` (`oidc` or
+`trustedProxy`). Put the selected provider mapping and static RBAC policy in a
+separate, non-secret values file. For example, an OIDC deployment can use:
+
+```yaml
+identity:
+  mode: oidc
+  config:
+    oidc:
+      issuer: https://idp.customer.example/
+      audience: lightwork
+      jwksUri: https://idp.customer.example/jwks.json
+      algorithms: [RS256, ES256]
+      clientId: lightwork-dashboard
+      redirectUri: https://lightwork.customer.example/auth/callback
+      authorizationEndpoint: ""
+      tokenEndpoint: ""
+    dashboard:
+      defaultRole: viewer
+      admins: ["user:break-glass-subject"]
+      defaultSuites: [finance]
+      groupRoles:
+        PlatformAdmins: admin
+      groupSuites:
+        Finance: [finance]
+    kernel:
+      roles:
+        restricted:
+          allowTools: [knowledge_search]
+          denyTools: [exec]
+          maxRisk: low
+          allowPaths: ["/workspace/**"]
+          allowHosts: []
+      roleAssignments:
+        default: restricted
+```
+
+A trusted-proxy deployment uses the same required dashboard/RBAC structure but
+replaces the OIDC block with only its selected provider block:
+
+```yaml
+identity:
+  mode: trustedProxy
+  config:
+    trustedProxy:
+      header: X-Authenticated-User
+      trustedProxies: [10.44.0.20, 10.44.1.0/24]
+      trustLoopback: false
+    dashboard:
+      defaultRole: viewer
+      admins: ["user:break-glass-subject"]
+      defaultSuites: []
+      groupRoles: {}
+      groupSuites: {}
+    kernel:
+      roles: {}
+      roleAssignments: {}
+```
+
+Production rejects unknown or credential-shaped identity fields, environment
+interpolation, unsafe proxy headers, loopback fallback, and any non-empty
+inactive-provider block. The chart renders the approved values as an immutable,
+content-addressed ConfigMap, mounts it read-only, and sets `MAVERICK_CONFIG` to
+that exact `config.toml`. Its checksum is present on the pod template and its
+values are covered by `MAVERICK_PRODUCTION_DEPLOYMENT_CONFIG_SHA256`. OIDC
+client/session secrets never enter this ConfigMap; in OIDC mode only, their two
+keys remain in the allowlisted Kubernetes Secret projection.
+
+Compute the canonical, non-secret identity/RBAC posture from the exact rendered
+runtime config:
+
+```bash
+MAVERICK_CONFIG=/path/to/extracted-rendered-config.toml \
+  python -c "from maverick.production_identity import runtime_identity_posture as p; print(p().sha256)"
+```
+
+The digest covers the selected provider, issuer/proxy identity mapping, built-in
+dashboard permissions, dashboard default/admin/group mappings, and configured
+kernel roles/assignments. OIDC client/session secret values are excluded, as are
+mutable user/tenant roster files; those require live qualification and
+authorized-change evidence rather than a one-time repository digest. Helm checks
+the digest's shape but does not duplicate the runtime's canonicalization logic:
+an arbitrary 64-hex value can render, but production startup rejects it unless
+it equals the posture derived from the mounted ConfigMap.
+
+The
+chart itself computes `MAVERICK_PRODUCTION_DEPLOYMENT_CONFIG_SHA256` as SHA-256
+over canonical JSON for the merged, runtime-relevant Helm values. It excludes
+only `productionOnboarding` and `secret.rotationGeneration`, which are
+separately bound trust/evidence transport fields; this avoids a circular digest
+when a new signed observation packet is staged. Then install an immutable image
+by digest:
 
 ```bash
-# Minimal install (SQLite, single replica). Provide a provider key + token.
