+### Postgres whole-database migration
+
+The serving role is deliberately unable to bypass forced tenant RLS, so it can
+only verify rows in its active tenant. A production migration must instead run
+from a drained, one-shot job using the separate table-owning migration role.
+Put that role's DSN in an owner-only custody file; never pass it as a command-line
+literal or mount it in a serving pod:
+
+```bash
+export MAVERICK_PG_MIGRATION_DSN_FILE=/var/run/secrets/lightwork-migrator/dsn
+maverick encryption migrate-postgres --dry-run
+maverick encryption migrate-postgres
+maverick encryption migrate-postgres --dry-run
+```
+
+The role must be `NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS`, own every sealed-field table, and
+connect to the exact current schema. The command takes an advisory lock plus
+access-exclusive locks on the sensitive tables, transactionally suspends forced
+RLS for the owner, authenticates every existing ciphertext under its row's
+tenant, rewrites legacy plaintext, verifies all rows a second time, restores RLS
+and immutable-record triggers, and only then commits. Any error rolls the whole
+operation back. The final command exits nonzero if any plaintext remains.
+
+Retain the JSON report with the release evidence. It contains no field values;
+it records the exact schema version, role, sealed-column manifest digest,
+per-column counts, completion time, and readiness result. Database snapshots and
+PITR remain the rollback mechanism; do not create a plaintext `pg_dump` merely
+for this rewrite.
