+## Emergency fleet halt
+
+Use `maverick halt --reason "incident reference"` or the authenticated
+dashboard Halt control. In production, a successful response means all four
+conditions completed: the local in-process/file fallback was armed first, the
+global Postgres halt row was committed and read back, the required global audit
+event was written, and the serving replica handed control to that shared row.
+In Postgres deployments the successful handoff removes the replica-local latch
+so a later clear may be served by any replica. SQLite keeps the HALT file as its
+process-shared authority because hot paths deliberately do not poll its local
+database row. If shared authority, audit, or handoff is unavailable,
+the command or API fails instead of claiming fleet success; the local stop
+remains armed as a safety fallback. Treat that response as an incomplete fleet
+arm and verify the other replicas directly.
+
+Admission, queue workers, flow nodes, LLM polling, and connector/tool dispatch
+force-refresh the halt authority and fail closed in production. Claimed worker
+attempts that lose an arm race are deferred without running the handler and
+write a typed terminal `cancellation` outcome. A halt cannot be consumed by a
+flow-authored catch branch. Already in-flight external mutations cannot be
+safely killed mid-request; they must finish or enter the normal indeterminate
+reconciliation path, and no later effect boundary is admitted.
+
+Clearing is intentionally stricter. `maverick unhalt` or the authenticated
+dashboard clear must supply an authenticated actor and an explicit authorization
+decision. The required audit record is written before shared disarm, and the
+shared row is deactivated only if its generation still matches the observed
+halt; its inactive tombstone preserves monotonic generations across later
+re-arms. SQLite v34 and Postgres v35 add compatibility triggers for rolling
+upgrades: a legacy replica cannot delete that tombstone or re-arm without
+advancing its generation; an old reader may remain conservatively halted until
+the replica is replaced.
+A concurrent re-arm returns a conflict and remains active. Local state is not
+cleared if authorization, audit, shared disarm, or final verification fails.
+Never remove the HALT file manually in a production fleet: that changes only one
+replica and bypasses the shared compare-and-swap and audit controls.
