+## Recovering pending approval and sign-off audits
+
+Production approval votes and deliverable sign-offs commit first to tenant-local
+transactional outboxes. They remain non-effective until their stable event ID
+is present in the required signed audit chain and the same database transaction
+finalizes the reviewed decision. A client does not need to remain connected:
+the dashboard drains both outboxes at startup, every minute through the reviewed
+`authority_audit_sweep` system job, and once more during bounded shutdown.
+
+`/readyz` fails and `/metrics` reports
+`maverick_authority_audit_backlog` whenever any recoverable tenant has pending
+authority-audit rows or the inventory is unreadable. Repair the audit signer,
+custody, storage, or database dependency; do not create a differently keyed
+replacement decision. Recovery retries the original stable event identity,
+marks superseded sign-offs stale, and never promotes an unaudited decision.
+
+## Tenant quota and backpressure recovery
+
+The admitted Postgres profile uses `tenant_quota_usage` plus durable
+`tenant_quota_reservations` as the only fleet admission authority. Configure
+all six ceilings through `MAVERICK_TENANT_QUOTA_LIMITS_JSON` (a `default`
+vector plus optional exact tenant overrides) or the scalar environment
+overrides documented in `docs/env-vars.md`. Set an explicit monotonic policy
+generation and increment it for every limit change. The generation, hash, and
+six typed limits are fenced in the same shared tenant row: stale replicas and
+same-generation disagreements refuse admission and make readiness fail. A
+production deployment validates its complete base vector before enumerating
+the first tenant, so an empty roster cannot conceal malformed or partial policy. A
+missing requested ceiling,
+unavailable Postgres transaction, malformed mutable policy, or mismatched
+tenant identity refuses new work; Lightwork never falls back to a per-host
+counter.
+
+Queue admission persists a stable `queued_jobs` lease identity on the job row;
+polling, claim, and heartbeat renew it, including after restart. Cap-blocked
+tenants are skipped so they cannot head-of-line block another tenant. Terminal
+queue states release the exact persisted hold and terminal jobs cannot be
+resurrected by stale failure callbacks. Run admission separately holds
+`concurrent_runs` and the run's maximum uncached-input-plus-output token,
+spend, and tool-call vector. Direct governed-flow actions reserve and settle
+one tool call. Structured deliverable artifacts reserve and settle their exact
+UTF-8 bytes. Run completion converts the projection to actual usage exactly
+once after finalization. A hard crash leaves the conservative hold active;
+expiry transactionally charges the cumulative projection exactly once before
+reopening occupancy. Reusing a request id with different
+principal or resource inputs is a policy error, while replaying the exact
+request returns the original row and cannot double-charge.
+
+Operational signals:
+
+- `/readyz` reports `production_tenant_quota_authority` and fails if an active
+  tenant cannot read the authority or lacks any required vector ceiling.
+- `/metrics` publishes `maverick_tenant_quota_authority_ready`,
+  `maverick_tenant_quota_policy_generation`, and
+  `maverick_tenant_quota_resource`. Tenant labels are stable SHA-256 prefixes,
+  never customer identifiers; resource and standing labels come from fixed
+  allow-lists.
+- `connector_calls` and `vector_store_bytes`, and attachment storage in the
+  first production profile, remain explicitly unavailable. Do not infer
+  enforcement from a zero metric.
+
+During an outage, restore the configured Postgres authority and verify the
+same policy document. Do not clear active rows manually: doing so can reopen a
+noisy-neighbor race while a provider or worker still owns admitted work. Live
+Postgres concurrency and failover behavior must be proven against the target
+managed service before release; SQLite tests prove only development parity.
